CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ more specialized chosen: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
Partial ordering deduced G's parameters from F's arguments but did not
check that the parts of G's arguments naming none of its parameters were
identical to F's: `int *' was taken to match `C *', so both partial
specializations were at least as specialized as each other and the first
one declared won.  A template-id with defaulted arguments omitted
(`equal_to<>') had fewer arguments than `equal_to<C>' and matched
nothing.
